---
title: Lakes
summary: Static HTML inside an island. A frozen region that ships zero client JS, although it lives in an interactive tree.
related:
  - regions/timing-nesting
  - regions/client-islands
---

A **lake** is static HTML *inside* an island. The island around it is interactive. The lake itself never ships JS. You mark it with `wake: 'none'`, inside the `.svelte` file of an island.

::: tip When to use
Use a lake for a **large static subtree inside an interactive component**: prose, a prerendered chart, a highlighted code block, an image gallery. The lake keeps that subtree out of the client bundle of the island. If the static content is *not* inside an island, it costs nothing already. Unmarked markup on a Page ships no JS. Lakes matter only inside a tree that hydrates.
:::

```svelte
<!-- inside an island -->
<script>
  import HeavyChart from './HeavyChart.svelte' with { wake: 'none' };
</script>

<section>
  <LiveControls />        <!-- interactive -->
  <HeavyChart data={rows} /> <!-- frozen: rendered once on the server, never hydrated -->
</section>
```

The lake renders on the server. When the island around it hydrates, ogygia lifts the DOM of the lake out, keeps it safe, and puts it back in place. Thus a costly static subtree stays free of JS inside a live component.

## Why not render it inline?

Inside an island that hydrates, ordinary markup compiles into the client bundle of the island. A lake removes a subtree from that bundle. Use it for content that is costly to ship but static: prose, a large prerendered chart, a code block with syntax colors.

The island below is interactive: the counter counts. The snapshot inside it is a lake: server HTML, lifted out and restored around the hydration of the island. It ships no JS of its own.

<script>
  import LakeDemo from '$lib/demos/LakeDemo.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
</script>

<div class="doc-demo">
  <LakeDemo />
</div>

## Keeping a lake fresh: render: live

By default, a lake is baked one time and restored unchanged. When its content must refresh in the background, make it `render: 'live'`. A live lake serves baked static content, then revalidates:

```svelte
<script>
  import HeavyChart from './HeavyChart.svelte' with { render: 'live', wake: 'visible' };
</script>

<HeavyChart data={rows} />
```

`wake` is the revalidate schedule (`load` / `idle` / `visible` / media). `maxAge` and `onExpire`, set in a preset, control staleness. `render: 'live'` serves the baked content immediately. It refreshes the content in the background through the signed island endpoint.

> After an SPA navigation, a lake restored inside kept layout chrome is marked as settled again. Thus an island nested in that lake can wake. See [SPA router](/docs/app/spa-router).

## Do / don't

- **Do** put your heaviest static content (long prose, large charts, code) in a lake when it is inside an island.
- **Do** use `render: 'live'` when the server HTML of a lake must refresh in the background.
- **Don't** put interactive UI in a lake. A lake ships no JS. Buttons and inputs in it are inert. Use a nested [client island](/docs/regions/client-islands) instead.


